00:38:57.300 --> 00:38:59.800
And we asked these to Bryan Cranston.

716
00:38:59.860 --> 00:39:03.800
And I was like, oh, my gosh, Jenna, we have to ask these to every guest moving forward because

717
00:39:03.800 --> 00:39:05.900
these questions are so fun.

718
00:39:06.180 --> 00:39:07.540
So here they are.

719
00:39:07.740 --> 00:39:12.160
The first question is, what was your first entertainment job?

720
00:39:12.780 --> 00:39:19.040
Oh, well, the first, like, real professional, I was 16.

721
00:39:19.580 --> 00:39:20.940
Yeah, 16 or 17.

722
00:39:21.220 --> 00:39:26.060
And I grew up in Vermont, the Bread Loaf Campus of Middlebury College in the summer.

723
00:39:26.060 --> 00:39:31.360
I would do a language program and they would also have people from theater in Providence

724
00:39:31.360 --> 00:39:32.500
come up and do shows.

725
00:39:32.680 --> 00:39:40.540
So I was in Tom Stoppard's Acadia when I was like 16, I think 17.

726
00:39:41.780 --> 00:39:45.120
And, you know, it's just for the summer, but was like working with professional actors

727
00:39:45.120 --> 00:39:47.600
and knew I wanted to be an actor at that point.

728
00:39:47.800 --> 00:39:50.240
So that was like my first ever.

729
00:39:51.160 --> 00:39:55.420
Oh, Jake, that makes my theater nerd heart swell so much.

730
00:39:56.120 --> 00:39:56.780
All right.

731
00:39:56.940 --> 00:39:58.320
I have the next question.

732
00:39:58.940 --> 00:40:00.960
Do you speak any other languages?

733
00:40:02.320 --> 00:40:02.800
No.

734
00:40:03.160 --> 00:40:04.200
Language of the heart.

735
00:40:04.520 --> 00:40:05.180
No, I don't.

736
00:40:05.320 --> 00:40:05.880
I don't.

737
00:40:06.220 --> 00:40:10.080
I'm working on Italian, but it's not going quickly.

738
00:40:10.960 --> 00:40:12.140
So no, the answer's no.

739
00:40:12.340 --> 00:40:14.380
What was the goal in wanting to learn Italian?

740
00:40:14.680 --> 00:40:19.780
Was it, I'm going to go there and I'm going to be, like, is what, why Italian?

741
00:40:20.920 --> 00:40:26.940
I spent a very small amount of time there maybe 10 years ago and traveled around in Italy and

742
00:40:26.940 --> 00:40:29.320
thought, like, this is it.

743
00:40:29.640 --> 00:40:32.600
This is where I would move tomorrow.

744
00:40:32.980 --> 00:40:34.040
Like, any part of it.

745
00:40:34.620 --> 00:40:36.860
Tuscany, Umbria, down to the coaSt.

746
00:40:36.860 --> 00:40:40.240
I mean, like, I just was totally taken with it.

747
00:40:42.500 --> 00:40:48.060
And, you know, always honestly have one foot in going, like, maybe I'll move to Italy.

748
00:40:48.520 --> 00:40:50.000
You know, like, I have kids now.

749
00:40:50.120 --> 00:40:50.760
I'm married, whatever.

750
00:40:50.880 --> 00:40:52.420
But go, like, let's go.

751
00:40:53.200 --> 00:40:54.920
Like, this business is so remote.

752
00:40:56.260 --> 00:40:58.460
I rarely shoot in New York or LA anyways.

753
00:40:58.860 --> 00:41:03.860
There's no reason for me to go, like, I need to be a train ride away from the city.

754
00:41:03.860 --> 00:41:06.780
It's like, yeah, if I want to do a play, that's true.

755
00:41:07.120 --> 00:41:14.220
But the reality of mostly what my work is, is, like, sending in self-tapes, meeting people over Zoom.

756
00:41:15.040 --> 00:41:19.300
You know, maybe an offer, but really that's kind of rare.

757
00:41:19.740 --> 00:41:21.140
So, like, I could live anywhere.

758
00:41:21.500 --> 00:41:24.640
And why not pick up and go to Florence?

759
00:41:24.960 --> 00:41:26.800
Why not go to Siena, you know?

760
00:41:27.260 --> 00:41:37.800
So, and if we were to do that, I'd like to be engaged in a culture and not just, like, an expat floating around the prototypical American to go, like, anyone here speak English?

761
00:41:38.100 --> 00:41:38.520
Right, right.

762
00:41:38.520 --> 00:41:39.260
English, anybody.

763
00:41:39.680 --> 00:41:40.280
But we'll see.

764
00:41:40.600 --> 00:41:46.280
Well, that folds into question number three on the call sheet is, what's a place you've been to that you've loved?

765
00:41:46.480 --> 00:41:50.100
So, I think that's for you, Italy?

766
00:41:50.540 --> 00:41:51.760
Italy, to be sure.

767
00:41:51.760 --> 00:41:52.820
And also, I mean, London.

768
00:41:53.060 --> 00:41:53.300
I don't know.

769
00:41:53.400 --> 00:41:54.220
I've spent a little time in London.

770
00:41:54.380 --> 00:41:59.320
I thought this is, I would also, you know, it's the most expensive city maybe in the world.

771
00:42:00.560 --> 00:42:03.060
But London I just thought was wonderful.

772
00:42:03.760 --> 00:42:04.100
Yeah.

773
00:42:04.620 --> 00:42:05.280
I don't know.

774
00:42:05.400 --> 00:42:07.300
I think that all sounds pretty fantastic.

775
00:42:07.700 --> 00:42:08.540
I want to go.

776
00:42:09.400 --> 00:42:10.640
You know, I had friends.

777
00:42:10.640 --> 00:42:11.980
I had friends in my 20s.

778
00:42:12.000 --> 00:42:16.380
Of course, they didn't have kids yet, you know, but they decided to go live in Italy for a year.

779
00:42:16.800 --> 00:42:19.680
And they went and they lived in Italy for a year and they loved it.

780
00:42:19.860 --> 00:42:23.240
And I think they think back to that time in their life so fondly.

781
00:42:23.400 --> 00:42:24.560
And I grew up overseas.

782
00:42:25.160 --> 00:42:27.200
So, Jake, I'm saying go for it.

783
00:42:27.560 --> 00:42:27.610
Yeah.

784
00:42:27.700 --> 00:42:28.460
Where did you grow up?

785
00:42:28.640 --> 00:42:29.500
Where were you overseas?

786
00:42:29.780 --> 00:42:32.100
I lived 12 years in Jakarta, Indonesia.

787
00:42:32.900 --> 00:42:33.840
Oh, my God.

788
00:42:34.040 --> 00:42:36.260
What was there that your family was there for?

789
00:42:36.440 --> 00:42:36.640
What was that?

790
00:42:36.700 --> 00:42:40.740
My dad was a drilling engineer and he was transferred there in the 70s.

791
00:42:40.860 --> 00:42:41.840
And I grew up there.

792
00:42:42.780 --> 00:42:44.760
I loved that chapter of my life.

793
00:42:44.880 --> 00:42:48.820
So, and I think you just, it's such an education.

794
00:42:48.880 --> 00:42:50.600
So, I don't want to get in trouble here.

795
00:42:50.700 --> 00:42:52.540
I don't want to step in your business with your wife.

796
00:42:52.740 --> 00:42:53.600
But I'm just saying.

797
00:42:53.700 --> 00:42:54.520
She's into it.

798
00:42:54.680 --> 00:42:55.560
She's like, let's go.

799
00:42:55.700 --> 00:42:56.120
I'm saying go for it.

800
00:42:56.380 --> 00:42:57.020
For real.

801
00:42:57.200 --> 00:42:57.340
Yeah.

802
00:42:57.440 --> 00:42:59.140
No, you're tipping the scale.

803
00:42:59.500 --> 00:43:03.480
Well, if office ladies come to Italy, we will find you.

804
00:43:03.720 --> 00:43:04.960
You've always got a place to sit.

805
00:43:05.100 --> 00:43:06.480
You'll always be welcome in our house.

806
00:43:07.400 --> 00:43:08.440
Pasta and pools.

807
00:43:09.400 --> 00:43:13.160
Next question is, what do you like to do on the weekends?

808
00:43:16.100 --> 00:43:19.400
I mean, I've got two little boys.

809
00:43:19.600 --> 00:43:22.600
So, it's, the weekends are not my own.

810
00:43:22.800 --> 00:43:23.280
Do you know what I mean?

811
00:43:23.340 --> 00:43:24.440
It's like when they're in school.

812
00:43:25.700 --> 00:43:28.680
On the weekends, what do I really like to do?

813
00:43:29.140 --> 00:43:32.240
If I get the chance, I love running.

814
00:43:32.500 --> 00:43:33.580
I love trail running.

815
00:43:33.580 --> 00:43:36.500
If I can get away for just a little bit to do that.

816
00:43:36.780 --> 00:43:39.520
Or, boy, reading.

817
00:43:39.700 --> 00:43:41.980
If I can get like a half hour in of just reading.

818
00:43:42.460 --> 00:43:43.920
Boy, that's a pleasure.

819
00:43:44.180 --> 00:43:46.700
You're so obviously the parent of small children.

820
00:43:47.820 --> 00:43:49.860
Because I remember these days.

821
00:43:50.340 --> 00:43:50.720
A shower.

822
00:43:51.020 --> 00:43:51.360
Yes.

823
00:43:51.680 --> 00:43:53.780
An uninterrupted shower.

824
00:43:54.080 --> 00:43:54.220
Yeah.

825
00:43:54.720 --> 00:43:54.960
Okay.

826
00:43:55.120 --> 00:43:56.720
Last question, Jake.

827
00:43:57.420 --> 00:44:00.280
What is your favorite midnight snack?

828
00:44:02.760 --> 00:44:03.580
Cold pizza.

829
00:44:04.840 --> 00:44:05.240
Oh, yeah.

830
00:44:05.880 --> 00:44:06.220
Oh, yeah.

831
00:44:06.760 --> 00:44:07.160
I'll eat.

832
00:44:07.160 --> 00:44:07.920
I'll order pizza.

833
00:44:08.600 --> 00:44:10.280
Eat too much of it the first time.

834
00:44:11.400 --> 00:44:13.000
Convince myself I won't have any later.

835
00:44:13.480 --> 00:44:14.480
Put the kids to bed.

836
00:44:14.680 --> 00:44:15.580
Put something on.

837
00:44:15.680 --> 00:44:16.620
Be like, do you want a snack?

838
00:44:17.320 --> 00:44:18.700
My wife's like, I'm good, dude.

839
00:44:18.720 --> 00:44:19.920
I had too much for dinner.

840
00:44:20.080 --> 00:44:20.880
I'm like, yeah, no, totally.

841
00:44:21.320 --> 00:44:23.620
And then go downstairs and like, make the coffee.

842
00:44:23.720 --> 00:44:24.400
Let the dog out.

843
00:44:24.560 --> 00:44:25.580
Eat two pieces of pizza.

844
00:44:25.860 --> 00:44:26.960
Be like, what are you doing?

845
00:44:27.260 --> 00:44:28.180
What is happening?

846
00:44:29.100 --> 00:44:29.920
So it's that.

847
00:44:30.100 --> 00:44:35.520
And also, man, of late, frosted mini wheats has been like, they're just in there.

848
00:44:35.540 --> 00:44:37.460
And I think, oh, that'll fill me up a little.

849
00:44:37.580 --> 00:44:38.300
It's a little sweet.

850
00:44:38.460 --> 00:44:39.540
It's a little whatever, you know.

851
00:44:39.720 --> 00:44:42.460
And like, we have these poor tubs.

852
00:44:42.580 --> 00:44:45.060
It's not just the box, you know, keeping it all real organized.

853
00:44:46.460 --> 00:44:53.160
And, but I won't like put it in a bowl because I'm convinced, you know, I'm convinced I'm going to have like two little handfuls.

854
00:44:53.160 --> 00:44:53.780
And that'll be that.

855
00:44:53.940 --> 00:44:58.120
But instead, I probably put away like half a bag of cereal and then go like, what?

856
00:44:58.320 --> 00:44:59.440
Dude, it's midnight.

857
00:44:59.960 --> 00:45:02.900
Like, come on, bud.

858
00:45:03.120 --> 00:45:04.440
Also, why run?

859
00:45:04.560 --> 00:45:04.980
You know what I mean?

860
00:45:05.120 --> 00:45:06.740
Just why run and go to the gym?

861
00:45:06.920 --> 00:45:11.920
Then from 11 to 12, you're going to do your own at home fourth meal.

862
00:45:12.400 --> 00:45:14.980
You know, remember when Taco Bell made that a thing?

863
00:45:15.220 --> 00:45:16.960
They were just legit like, go ahead.

864
00:45:17.080 --> 00:45:19.940
Have 25% more food than anyone's ever recommended.

865
00:45:20.220 --> 00:45:20.640
Fourth meal.

866
00:45:20.940 --> 00:45:21.380
Oh.

867
00:45:22.720 --> 00:45:23.360
All right.

868
00:45:23.560 --> 00:45:23.900
Cool.

869
00:45:25.060 --> 00:45:25.380
Yeah.

870
00:45:25.600 --> 00:45:26.360
We'll do that.

871
00:45:27.080 --> 00:45:27.840
We're into it.

872
00:45:28.600 --> 00:45:29.300
That's very funny.

873
00:45:29.420 --> 00:45:33.800
So that's pizza, pizza, roasted mini-wheats, and yeah.

874
00:45:37.070 --> 00:45:37.530
Nutritious.

875
00:45:38.150 --> 00:45:41.630
Super, you're packing in some extra nutrition.

876
00:45:42.630 --> 00:45:45.130
I love not committing to a bowl, though.

877
00:45:45.310 --> 00:45:45.910
That is me.

878
00:45:45.990 --> 00:45:49.090
I'm like, if I only take a few handfuls of M&Ms, is it really anything?

879
00:45:49.410 --> 00:45:50.890
Because I didn't put it in a container.

880
00:45:51.290 --> 00:45:52.150
It's just a few.

881
00:45:52.150 --> 00:45:52.750
Right.

882
00:45:53.070 --> 00:45:53.810
And I doubled it.

883
00:45:54.150 --> 00:45:54.590
Yeah.

884
00:45:55.070 --> 00:45:55.490
Yeah.

885
00:45:55.610 --> 00:45:58.930
And then by October 10th, you're like, somehow we're out of candy for Halloween.

886
00:45:59.190 --> 00:46:01.110
I don't know how that happened, but.

887
00:46:02.710 --> 00:46:03.410
Wasn't me.

888
00:46:03.550 --> 00:46:04.510
I didn't put it in a bowl.

889
00:46:05.130 --> 00:46:05.570
Yeah.

890
00:46:05.750 --> 00:46:05.930
No.

891
00:46:06.290 --> 00:46:07.110
It's, yeah, it's a problem.

892
00:46:07.310 --> 00:46:07.730
It's a problem.

893
00:46:08.410 --> 00:46:09.110
Aw, Jake.

894
00:46:09.110 --> 00:46:12.330
It was so good to see you and get to reconnect.

895
00:46:12.370 --> 00:46:13.890
You are just the same.

896
00:46:14.470 --> 00:46:14.910
Thank you.

897
00:46:14.910 --> 00:46:16.710
Just the same awesome guy.

898
00:46:17.230 --> 00:46:18.510
And this was really fun.

899
00:46:19.110 --> 00:46:19.310
Yeah.

900
00:46:19.310 --> 00:46:19.890
I really, yeah.

901
00:46:20.030 --> 00:46:20.510
Thanks so much.

902
00:46:20.570 --> 00:46:21.310
This was really lovely.

903
00:46:21.570 --> 00:46:23.190
Thank you for having me on here.

904
00:46:23.410 --> 00:46:27.050
And Jake, we just loved you on set.

905
00:46:27.550 --> 00:46:30.990
We, you were just so wonderful to be around.

906
00:46:30.990 --> 00:46:36.850
And so I just hope you know how much we all just like, you, you just fit in seamlessly

907
00:46:36.850 --> 00:46:38.610
and you were just part of the family.

908
00:46:39.270 --> 00:46:41.070
That really means a lot.

909
00:46:41.450 --> 00:46:41.850
Thank you.

910
00:46:42.350 --> 00:46:42.870
Thank you.

911
00:46:43.330 --> 00:46:45.390
This is like me then saying thank you.

912
00:46:45.390 --> 00:46:48.770
You and me now saying thank you, you know, like it really, thanks.

913
00:46:49.430 --> 00:46:49.950
Thank you.

914
00:46:51.450 --> 00:46:52.790
And yeah, thank you.

915
00:46:58.980 --> 00:47:00.120
Do you see what we're saying?

916
00:47:00.300 --> 00:47:01.480
How great is Jake Lacey?

917
00:47:01.700 --> 00:47:02.120
I know.

918
00:47:02.500 --> 00:47:03.080
Love him.

919
00:47:03.180 --> 00:47:04.540
I love him even more now.

920
00:47:04.680 --> 00:47:05.000
I know.

921
00:47:05.560 --> 00:47:09.060
Well, a big thanks to Jake for sharing with us about his time on The Office.

922
00:47:09.280 --> 00:47:12.880
And please be sure to check out his new show, Apples Never Fall on Peacock.

923
00:47:13.120 --> 00:47:14.340
Thanks so much for listening.

924
00:47:14.560 --> 00:47:16.780
We hope you have a good week and we'll see you next time.

925
00:47:16.940 --> 00:47:17.340
See you then.

926
00:47:17.340 --> 00:47:23.110
Thank you for listening to Office Ladies.

927
00:47:23.390 --> 00:47:27.530
Office Ladies is produced by Earwolf, Jenna Fisher, and Angela Kinsey.

928
00:47:27.750 --> 00:47:30.030
Our senior producer is Cassie Jerkins.

929
00:47:30.230 --> 00:47:32.310
Our audio engineer is Jordan Duffy.

930
00:47:32.570 --> 00:47:34.750
And our associate producer is Ainsley Bubaco.

931
00:47:35.090 --> 00:47:38.010
Our theme song is Rubber Tree by Creed Bratton.
